WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.189
Governing MCP for a
workforce the size of a city.

00:00:03.367 --> 00:00:07.281
So how many people are using MCP in
this workforce? Let me put it in ships.

00:00:07.467 --> 00:00:09.773
An Enterprise's worth: four
hundred and twenty-eight?

00:00:09.967 --> 00:00:12.075
The Enterprise-D, about a thousand?

00:00:12.267 --> 00:00:13.973
A Battlestar's worth,
twenty-eight hundred?

00:00:14.167 --> 00:00:17.011
A Halo's worth, seventeen thousand?

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.632
A Star Destroyer, thirty-seven thousand?

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.192
The Executor, two hundred
and eighty thousand?

00:00:22.367 --> 00:00:24.428
The Death Star, one point two million?

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.353
I am not supposed to say the exact number.

00:00:27.353 --> 00:00:32.595
But it is about two-thirds of a Death
Star, actively using MCP every single day.

00:00:32.833 --> 00:00:36.049
You might think I am going
to do a technical keynote.

00:00:36.049 --> 00:00:40.660
We are at a tech conference. But my
message today is remarkably untechnical.

00:00:40.833 --> 00:00:44.647
You expect the number, the architecture,
the process, the security issues,

00:00:44.647 --> 00:00:46.658
and the things we thought were unusual.

00:00:46.833 --> 00:00:50.451
People have covered those all
week, and it is all on the website.

00:00:50.633 --> 00:00:54.783
What I really want you to leave with is
the most effective lever for MCP adoption.

00:00:54.783 --> 00:00:57.971
It was not what I expected. Before
that, the things you expect.

00:00:58.233 --> 00:01:01.958
The architecture, the simple
version. A person. A device.

00:01:01.958 --> 00:01:04.049
An agent running on that device.

00:01:04.049 --> 00:01:08.492
One gate, a proxy and a registry,
that every tool call goes through.

00:01:08.492 --> 00:01:10.060
And the tools behind it.

00:01:10.233 --> 00:01:12.779
Done properly: the managed
device with its policies,

00:01:12.779 --> 00:01:15.081
tool traffic through the
proxy and the gateway,

00:01:15.081 --> 00:01:18.949
model traffic through its own gateway,
and the control plane feeding all of it.

00:01:19.133 --> 00:01:21.801
Now look at the two red
dashed lines at the bottom.

00:01:21.801 --> 00:01:25.305
The shell and the browser never
touch any of it. Hold that thought.

00:01:25.567 --> 00:01:28.196
Our process for accepting
a server: six gates.

00:01:28.367 --> 00:01:29.926
Do we have a relationship with the vendor?

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:31.458
Is there a real business need?

00:01:31.633 --> 00:01:33.978
Is it well built, and
wrapped where it falls short?

00:01:34.167 --> 00:01:35.250
Does it speak the current spec?

00:01:35.433 --> 00:01:37.443
Does it meet our security standards?

00:01:37.633 --> 00:01:39.104
And is the vendor certified?

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:42.152
The complete checklist, with
every source, is on the site.

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:48.774
One security tale that
surprised us. CVE-2026-47250.

00:01:48.774 --> 00:01:51.594
One line in a log file.

00:01:51.767 --> 00:01:52.912
An attacker plants it.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.128
An operator asks the
agent to read the logs,

00:01:55.128 --> 00:01:58.677
and it feeds the planted instruction to
the agent along with everything else.

00:01:58.867 --> 00:02:01.533
The agent runs kubectl
against the attacker's server,

00:02:01.533 --> 00:02:03.294
with TLS verification switched off.

00:02:03.467 --> 00:02:06.844
kubectl sends the operator's
bearer token with the request.

00:02:07.033 --> 00:02:10.349
The attacker now holds a token for
a cluster they could not reach.

00:02:10.533 --> 00:02:14.087
A hacker's game is multi-dimensional
chess: gather everything now,

00:02:14.087 --> 00:02:17.426
and the day that cluster is
exposed, they have one more piece.

00:02:17.667 --> 00:02:19.329
And things we thought were unusual.

00:02:19.329 --> 00:02:22.607
We wrapped MCP servers in MCP servers,
and we thought that was weird.

00:02:22.607 --> 00:02:24.175
Apparently everybody is doing it.

00:02:24.367 --> 00:02:26.644
The server does not meet
our security standards.

00:02:26.833 --> 00:02:29.621
Credentials must not cross
from client to server.

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:32.557
A hundred tools offered,
and nine presented.

00:02:32.733 --> 00:02:34.906
Reads and writes split
into separate servers.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:36.064
The wrong transport.

00:02:36.233 --> 00:02:37.749
Or every call logged.

00:02:37.933 --> 00:02:40.281
The full list, and the
tools, are on the site.

00:02:40.533 --> 00:02:44.952
But it is not the architecture or the
gating process I really want to talk

00:02:44.952 --> 00:02:48.653
about. What if you gave your end
users generative AI, and MCP,

00:02:48.833 --> 00:02:50.506
and said: plug them in like USB,

00:02:50.506 --> 00:02:53.327
sprinkle them everywhere
like your favorite hot sauce,

00:02:53.327 --> 00:02:56.045
and relentlessly use AI,
because that is how we win?

00:02:56.233 --> 00:02:59.859
You handed everyone a relentless
hacker, on your hardware,

00:02:59.859 --> 00:03:03.922
inside your security zones, with
your AI accounts and your tools.

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:07.863
And it runs on the end-user
device, authenticated as that user.

00:03:07.863 --> 00:03:10.253
People forget that a laptop is a server.

00:03:10.433 --> 00:03:13.684
So what happens when you tell
people with superpowers no?

00:03:13.933 --> 00:03:18.285
Then the user made a reasonable request.
I want a tool that reads my mail.

00:03:18.285 --> 00:03:18.932
No problem.

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:22.950
Draft replies for me? Not
entirely comfortable, but fine.

00:03:23.133 --> 00:03:25.579
Send email as me? Whoa. Hold on.

00:03:25.767 --> 00:03:28.379
These are human tools
for human communication.

00:03:28.379 --> 00:03:32.638
A bot has to be labeled as a bot,
secured as a bot, given its own identity.

00:03:32.638 --> 00:03:34.172
Nobody has solved that yet.

00:03:34.367 --> 00:03:37.012
So we said no, and we
offered alternatives.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:40.831
And the user asked the agent: I do
not see an MCP server for Outlook.

00:03:40.831 --> 00:03:42.989
Is there some other way we might do this?

00:03:43.233 --> 00:03:44.739
The agent wrote PowerShell.

00:03:44.739 --> 00:03:49.090
Classic Outlook has had an automation
interface for decades, and on a healthy,

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:51.767
managed laptop the script
went straight through.

00:03:51.767 --> 00:03:53.663
It read and sent mail as the user.

00:03:53.833 --> 00:03:56.345
Then they asked about
Teams. We said no again.

00:03:56.345 --> 00:03:59.511
So they put the approved browser
in remote debugging mode,

00:03:59.511 --> 00:04:02.951
and the agent walked the browser,
and the browser walked Teams.

00:04:02.951 --> 00:04:05.299
It never opened Teams.
It opened a browser.

00:04:05.467 --> 00:04:07.302
Remember the two red dashed lines?

00:04:07.302 --> 00:04:09.839
The shell and the browser
never touch the gate.

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:14.053
So what was the biggest lever?
The policy? The architecture?

00:04:14.053 --> 00:04:17.216
The proxies, the registries?
None of that alone.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:20.021
We had to win the hearts
and minds of users,

00:04:20.021 --> 00:04:23.715
because for the first time an
internal team is a SaaS company,

00:04:23.715 --> 00:04:26.337
and its biggest threat
is its own end users.

00:04:26.337 --> 00:04:29.912
If you do not get their buy-in,
they ask the agent to do it.

00:04:29.912 --> 00:04:31.699
That is how you get shadow IT.

00:04:31.867 --> 00:04:35.690
So love your users. Figure out what
they need, and get them to yes.

00:04:35.867 --> 00:04:38.191
Say no when you must, and explain why.

00:04:38.191 --> 00:04:41.433
A no without a why is a
workaround waiting to happen.

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:43.960
Send the signals: let
them vote, let them ask,

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:46.063
let them tell you where the openings are.

00:04:46.233 --> 00:04:50.482
And run the service like a product:
a self-service form, a feedback path,

00:04:50.482 --> 00:04:53.857
a leaderboard, communities of
practice, done relentlessly.

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:55.282
Talk to your users.

00:04:55.467 --> 00:04:59.119
The architecture, the policies,
the process: yes, all of that.

00:04:59.119 --> 00:05:03.536
But the biggest lever is a relationship
with the users who consume your MCP

00:05:03.536 --> 00:05:04.008
servers.

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:07.491
Talk and listen. It is not
just a technology problem.

00:05:07.491 --> 00:05:11.837
More than ever it is a human problem,
and humans can help us solve it.

00:05:12.033 --> 00:05:16.291
The details are at mcp dot michael
rishi forrester dot com. Come find me.

